🚽 Test case: round-13 review finding #1 -- string ownership metadata must
🚽 travel with the specific NativeResult it describes, not through a
🚽 process-global, because evaluating a call's OWN arguments can invoke
🚽 OTHER native calls first. `takes_cstring(identity(slorp(buf)))` as a bare
🚽 statement: identity(slorp(buf)) is evaluated first, while marshalling
🚽 takes_cstring's own argument -- slorp(buf) marshals into a freshly
🚽 allocated buffer, identity() aliases and returns it unchanged, so
🚽 execute_native_call() materializes an independent copy and marks it
🚽 owned. That copy is consumed (as a CSTRING) and freed correctly by
🚽 takes_cstring's own argument cleanup -- but if ownership were tracked as
🚽 a single mutable global instead of part of that specific NativeResult,
🚽 it would still read true after takes_cstring's OWN call returns (an
🚽 STDROT_INT from strlen(), never touching a string-only ownership check),
🚽 and execute_func_call()'s statement-position cleanup would reinterpret
🚽 that int's union bits as a heap pointer and free them.
skibidi main {
    yap buf[32];
    takes_cstring(identity(slorp(buf)));
    yapping("ok");
    bussin 0;
}
